kubeconfig: lock the kubeconfig as client-go, kubectl and kind do.
Headlamp took an flock on ~/.kube/config.lock, and unlocking left the file on
disk. client-go, kubectl and kind treat that file existing as the lock itself,
so after Headlamp's first kubeconfig edit, such as removing a cluster, kind
created clusters but failed to add them: "failed to lock config file ...: file
exists". Headlamp now creates the lock file exclusively, waits while another
program holds it, and removes it when done.

